WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:06.136
It had been a long, unsuccessful night, and 18-year-old Elvis Presley was frustrated.

2
00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:14.628
This session was his big chance to break into the recording business, but no matter how hard he tried, he couldn’t please the studio owner.

3
00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:22.509
Everyone took a break. Elvis sang one of his favorite songs to relax, and the guitar and bass players played along for fun.

4
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:28.954
When the studio owner heard them, he knew this fast-paced, energetic sound was exactly what he wanted!

5
00:00:28.955 --> 00:00:35.556
Elvis had been trying too hard to imitate big singers of the day, when all he needed to do was be himself.

6
00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:41.927
By being true to the music he loved, Elvis Presley would eventually become the king of rock and roll.

7
00:00:41.928 --> 00:00:49.212
Elvis was born on January 8, 1935, in a humble two-room house in Tupelo, Mississippi.

8
00:00:49.213 --> 00:00:55.323
His father, Vernon Presley, struggled to find work and was jailed for eight months for forging a check.

9
00:00:55.324 --> 00:01:00.749
Afterward, the family had an even harder time making ends meet and had to move often.

10
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:08.438
Between the frequent moves, an overprotective mother, and his father’s shameful prison record, Elvis always felt different.

11
00:01:08.438 --> 00:01:11.292
He was a shy boy who had few friends.

12
00:01:11.293 --> 00:01:19.181
"All I looked forward to was Sunday, when we could all go to church," he later said. There, he could do what he loved: sing.

13
00:01:19.182 --> 00:01:25.450
When his parents bought him a guitar for his eleventh birthday, he learned to accompany himself when he sang.

14
00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:33.886
In 1948, the family moved to Memphis, and Elvis’ life slowly improved. Vernon found a job driving delivery trucks.

15
00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:37.725
Elvis started to enjoy the attention he received for being different.

16
00:01:37.726 --> 00:01:43.708
Most boys had short hair, but Elvis wore his hair long "because that was the way the truck drivers wore it."

17
00:01:43.709 --> 00:01:49.609
Elvis also attracted attention because he dressed in the fancy clothes favored by African Americans.

18
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:57.491
After he won his high school’s talent contest, in which he sang and played guitar, Elvis finally gained some acceptance from his peers.

19
00:01:57.492 --> 00:02:04.928
"It was amazing how popular I became after that," he said. Best of all for Elvis, Memphis was a musical city.

20
00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:11.196
He loved the gospel music he heard in his church, as well as the gospel music of African American churches.

21
00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:18.791
He listened to country tunes by white performers on the radio and to the rhythm and blues played by local African American musicians.

22
00:02:18.792 --> 00:02:23.755
Elvis’ rise to fame and fortune began just after he graduated from high school.

23
00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:31.232
He paid four dollars to make a vocal recording at the small Sun Records studio&#8212;supposedly as a birthday gift for his mother.

24
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:37.342
The studio’s owner liked what he heard and eventually matched Elvis up with a bass player and a guitarist.

25
00:02:37.343 --> 00:02:44.445
From 1954 to 1955, the group recorded five singles and performed all over the South.

26
00:02:44.446 --> 00:02:52.437
Elvis named their fusion of country, gospel, pop, and blues "rockabilly," but the rest of the world called it "rock and roll."

27
00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:58.992
In August 1955, Elvis signed on with "Colonel" Tom Parker, a country-music promoter.

28
00:02:58.993 --> 00:03:07.215
Within a year, the Colonel had gotten Elvis a recording contract with RCA, a big record company, as well as a movie contract.

29
00:03:07.216 --> 00:03:17.416
Maybe it was nerves, or that he needed to keep time to the fast beat, but whenever Elvis sang, he shook his hips in a way that made young audiences go wild.

30
00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:25.234
For this, Elvis got the nickname "Elvis the Pelvis," which he hated. He couldn’t go anywhere without attracting fans.

31
00:03:25.235 --> 00:03:29.795
After one show, girls mobbed him and tore his clothing to shreds.

32
00:03:29.796 --> 00:03:36.460
Not everybody liked Elvis’ uninhibited performances, however. Older people called Elvis vulgar.

33
00:03:36.461 --> 00:03:42.395
Of course, that made teenagers love him more&#8212;because to them, Elvis symbolized rebellion.

34
00:03:42.396 --> 00:03:47.329
One fan recalled, "Hearing him for the first time was like busting out of jail."

35
00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:58.715
On his last appearance on Ed Sullivan’s weekly TV show, in January 1957, network censors ordered cameramen to avoid Elvis’ hips; they shot him from the waist up.

36
00:03:58.716 --> 00:04:08.423
At the same time, Ed Sullivan calmed fears that Elvis was a bad influence when the respected host told America that Elvis was "a real decent, fine boy."

37
00:04:08.424 --> 00:04:13.762
Elvis received fifty thousand dollars for his appearances on the show, a record-setting fee.

38
00:04:13.763 --> 00:04:22.818
This sum, plus the income from his hit records and other appearances, enabled Elvis to buy Graceland, an 18-room mansion in Memphis.

39
00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:28.523
Elvis seemed unstoppable, with his music and movies making him an international star.

40
00:04:28.524 --> 00:04:34.403
But the United States Army interrupted his career when they drafted him in December 1957.

41
00:04:34.404 --> 00:04:41.681
By the time Elvis left in the spring of 1958, he had recorded enough songs to stay in the news while in the service.

42
00:04:41.682 --> 00:04:50.349
In the army, he refused any special treatment and served with a unit based in Germany. In 1960, Elvis was honorably discharged.

43
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:59.899
Focusing on his movie career instead of touring, he became a wholesome, patriotic package that both teens and parents could like&#8212;and an even bigger star.

44
00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:08.321
In his movies, Elvis always appeared likeable, carefree, and graceful, and soundtracks released for the films kept Elvis’ songs on the radio.

45
00:05:08.322 --> 00:05:15.661
Critics, however, claimed Elvis sold out by starring in movies that quickly made huge sums of money but lacked artistic value.

46
00:05:15.662 --> 00:05:25.983
Some fans claim Colonel Parker exploited Elvis, turning down excellent parts in serious films and refusing to pay for good songwriters, in order to make a bigger profit.

47
00:05:25.984 --> 00:05:30.360
Elvis later admitted, "I sure lost my musical direction in Hollywood.

48
00:05:30.361 --> 00:05:35.587
My songs were the same conveyor belt mass production, just like most of my movies."

49
00:05:35.588 --> 00:05:43.468
In 1968, in order to resurrect his sagging career, Elvis did a TV special in which he sang before a live audience.

50
00:05:43.469 --> 00:05:50.063
His performance revealed intensity, humor, emotion, and confidence&#8212;reminding people why they loved him.

51
00:05:50.064 --> 00:05:57.411
One music critic later wrote, "There is something magical about watching a man who has lost himself find his way back home."

52
00:05:57.412 --> 00:06:01.669
Elvis left Hollywood and vowed not to record songs he didn’t believe in.

53
00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:12.610
Instead, he began a series of record-setting live performances in Las Vegas and other concerts across the United States, remaining a box-office sensation through the mid 1970s.

54
00:06:12.611 --> 00:06:19.648
But the work was exhausting, and Elvis took prescription drugs to pep him up before shows and to help him sleep afterward.

55
00:06:19.649 --> 00:06:28.808
He became addicted, and the heavy drug use hurt his body; his weight went up and down, and his behavior changed as his moods swung violently.

56
00:06:28.809 --> 00:06:37.587
His sudden death in his Graceland home on August 16, 1977, was attributed to a heart attack following a lethal mix of drugs.

57
00:06:37.588 --> 00:06:41.744
Since his death, Elvis Presley’s popularity has only increased.

58
00:06:41.745 --> 00:06:48.919
Graceland opened to the public in 1982 and has become one of the most frequented tourist sites in the United States.

59
00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:56.745
A series of mysterious Elvis sightings started rumors that he had faked his own death to escape the pressures of stardom and was still alive.

60
00:06:56.746 --> 00:07:06.897
These were probably sightings of Elvis impersonators, who had begun imitating the 1970s version of the king of rock and roll and continue to attract audiences across the globe.

61
00:07:06.898 --> 00:07:13.175
While it’s unlikely Elvis is still physically alive, he does live on in the hearts of people everywhere.

